<TABLE cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D580 border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR>
    <TD style=3D"BACKGROUND-COLOR: rgb(237,239,242)" =
width=3D29>&nbsp;</TD>
    <TD=20
    style=3D"FONT-SIZE: 13px; COLOR: rgb(51,51,51); FONT-FAMILY: =
Arial,Verdana,Helvetica,Sans-serif; BACKGROUND-COLOR: rgb(237,239,242)"=20
    vAlign=3Dtop width=3D"100%"><BR><BR>A design pattern is a general =
solution to=20
      a common problem in software design. The idea is that the solution =
gets=20
      translated into code, and that the code can be applied in =
different=20
      situations where the problem occurs. Discussion of design patterns =
started=20
      with the book <EM><A=20
      style=3D"FONT-SIZE: 13px; COLOR: rgb(153,153,204); FONT-FAMILY: =
Arial,Verdana,Helvetica,Sans-serif; TEXT-DECORATION: none"=20
      href=3D"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0201633612/">Design =
Patterns:=20
      Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software</A></EM> by Erich =
Gamma,=20
      Richard Helm, Ralph Johnson, and John Vlissides. These four =
authors are=20
      known as the "Gang of Four" or simply "GoF". In their book, the =
GoF=20
      categorized patterns into different subject areas, of which there =
are=20
      three primary areas: creational, structural, and behavioral. =
Creational=20
      patterns describe how objects are created (or "instantiated" in=20
      object-oriented terminology). Structural patterns offer help in =
how to=20
      connect and combine objects. Behavioral patterns describe =
algorithms or=20
      communication mechanisms. Some common pattern names are Singleton =
in the=20
      creational area, Observer in the behavioral area, and Facade in =
the=20
      structural area. This Tech Tip describes the Singleton pattern =
strictly in=20
      the context of the Java programming language. <BR><BR>One of the =
commonly=20
      used creational patterns is the Singleton pattern. It describes a=20
      technique for ensuring that only a single instance of a class is =
ever=20
      created. In essence, the technique takes the following approach: =
don't let=20
      anyone outside the class create instances of the object. =
Typically,=20
      Singletons are lazily created to reduce memory requirements until =
needed.=20
      You can implement this approach in many different ways. <BR><BR>If =
you=20
      know the one instance being created will be a subclass, make the =
parent=20
      class abstract and provide a method to get the current instance. =
An=20
      example of this is the <CODE>Toolkit</CODE> class in the AWT =
package. The=20
      constructor for <CODE>Toolkit</CODE> is public (the default =
constructor in=20
      this particular case): <BR><PRE>   public Toolkit()
</PRE>and the class has a <CODE>getDefaultToolkit()</CODE> method for=20
      getting the specific subclass -- in this case, the subclass is=20
      platform-specific: <BR><PRE>   public static Toolkit =
getDefaultToolkit()
</PRE>On a Linux platform with the Sun Java runtime, the specific =
subclass=20
      is of type <CODE>sun.awt.X11.XToolkit</CODE>. However you don't =
need to=20
      know that because you only access the class through its common =
abstract=20
      parent class, <CODE>Toolkit</CODE>. <BR><BR>The =
<CODE>Collator</CODE>=20
      class is another example of this pattern, with a slight =
difference. It=20
      offers two <CODE>getInstance()</CODE> methods. The no-argument =
version=20
      gets the <CODE>Collator</CODE> for the default locale. You can =
pass in=20
      your own locale to get the instance of the <CODE>Collator</CODE> =
for that=20
      locale. Request the <CODE>Collator</CODE> for the same locale =
multiple=20
      times and you get back the same <CODE>Collator</CODE> instance. =
The=20
      constructor itself is protected. Similar ways of restricting class =

      creation can be found throughout the J2SE standard libraries. =
<BR><BR>At=20
      this point you might think that restricting access to the =
constructor of a=20
      class automatically makes it a Singleton. It doesn't. A case in =
point is=20
      the <CODE>Calendar</CODE> class. The <CODE>Calendar</CODE> class=20
      constructor is protected, and the class offers a=20
      <CODE>getInstance()</CODE> method to get an instance of the class. =

      However, each call to <CODE>getInstance()</CODE> gets a new =
instance of=20
      the class. So that isn't a Singleton. <BR><BR>When you create your =
own=20
      Singleton class, make sure that only a single instance is ever =
created:=20
      <BR><PRE>   public class MySingleton {
     private static final MySingleton INSTANCE =3D=20
       new MySingleton();

     private MySingleton() {
     }

     public static final MySingleton getInstance() {
       return INSTANCE;
     }
   }
</PRE>The static method, <CODE>getInstance()</CODE>, returns the single=20
      instance of the class. Note that even if the single instance needs =
to be a=20
      subclass, you don't have to change the API. <BR><BR>Theoretically, =
you=20
      don't need the <CODE>getInstance()</CODE> method because the=20
      <CODE>INSTANCE</CODE> variable could be public. However, the=20
      <CODE>getInstance()</CODE> method does provide flexibility in case =
of=20
      future system changes. Good virtual machine implementations should =
inline=20
      the call to the static <CODE>getInstance()</CODE> method. =
<BR><BR>That's=20
      not quite all there is to creating a Singleton. If you need to =
make your=20
      Singleton class <CODE>Serializable</CODE>, you must provide a=20
      <CODE>readResolve()</CODE> method: <BR><PRE>  /**
   * Ensure Singleton class
   */
  private Object readResolve() throws ObjectStreamException {
    return INSTANCE;
  }
</PRE>With the <CODE>readResolve()</CODE> method in place, =
deserialization=20
      results in the one (and only one) object -- the same object as =
produced by=20
      calls to the <CODE>getInstance()</CODE> method. If you don't =
provide a=20
      <CODE>readResolve()</CODE> method, an instance of the object is =
created=20
      each time you deserialize the object. <BR><BR>The Singleton =
pattern is=20
      useful if you know you only have a single resource, and need to =
share=20
      access to the state information of that single resource. =
Identifying the=20
      need for the Singleton pattern at design time can simplify =
development.=20
      However, sometimes you're not aware of the need until a =
performance=20
      problem leads you to refactor code and use the pattern at a later =
time.=20
      For example, you might discover that system performance is =
degrading=20
      because your program is repeatedly creating instances of the same =
class to=20
      pass along state information. By changing to the Singleton =
pattern, you=20
      avoid recreating the same object. This frees up time the system =
uses to=20
      recreate the object, and saves the time the garbage collector =
needs to=20
      free those instances. <BR><BR>In short, use the Singleton design =
pattern=20
      when you want to ensure that one, and only one, instance of a =
class is=20
      ever created. If your constructor doesn't require any operations, =
provide=20
      an empty private constructor (or a protected constructor if you =
need to=20
      subclass). Otherwise, by default, the system will provide a public =

      constructor, something you don't want when working with a =
Singleton.=20
      <BR><BR>Note that Singletons are only guaranteed to be unique =
within a=20
      given class loader. If you use the same class across multiple =
distinct=20
      enterprise containers, you'll get one instance for each container. =

      <BR><BR>A Singleton pattern is often used with another pattern =
called the=20
      Factory pattern. Like the Singleton pattern, the Factory pattern =
is a=20
      creational pattern. It describes how subclasses of a particular =
object, or=20
      more typically, implementers of a particular interface, do the =
actual=20
      object creation. A good example of the Factory pattern is the =
Swing=20
      <CODE>BorderFactory</CODE> class. The class has a series of static =
methods=20
      returning different types of <CODE>Border</CODE> objects. It hides =
the=20
      implementation details of the subclasses, allowing the factory to =
directly=20
      call the constructors for the interface implementations. Here's an =
example=20
      of <CODE>BorderFactory</CODE> in use: <BR><PRE>   Border line =3D =
BorderFactory.createLineBorder(Color.RED);
   JLabel label =3D new JLabel("Red Line");
   label.setBorder(line);
</PRE>Here, the fact that <CODE>BorderFactory</CODE> creates a=20
      <CODE>LineBorder</CODE>, or how <CODE>BorderFactory</CODE> does =
that, is=20
      hidden from the developer. In this particular example, you can =
directly=20
      call the <CODE>LineBorder</CODE> constructor, but in many cases of =
using=20
      the Factory pattern, you can't. <BR><BR>Frequently, the class =
implementing=20
      the Singleton pattern returns an object to use as a Factory to =
create=20
      instances of a different class. This is exemplified by the=20
      <CODE>PopupFactory</CODE> class in the way it creates =
<CODE>Popup</CODE>=20
      objects. <BR><BR>To get the Singleton factory, you call the=20
      <CODE>getSharedInstance()</CODE> method of =
<CODE>PopupFactory</CODE>: <BR><PRE>   PopupFactory factory =3D =
PopupFactory.getSharedInstance();
</PRE>Then you create a <CODE>Popup</CODE> object from the factory by=20
      calling the factory's <CODE>getPopup()</CODE> method, passing in =
the=20
      parent component, its contents, and position: <BR><PRE>   Popup =
popup =3D factory.getPopup(owner, contents, x, y);
</PRE>You'll find the Factory pattern used frequently in a security=20
      context. In the following example, a certificate factory is =
obtained for a=20
      particular algorithm, then a certificate for a stream is =
generated: <BR><PRE>   FileInputStream fis =3D new =
FileInputStream(filename);
   CertificateFactory cf =3D=20
      CertificateFactory.getInstance("X.509");
   Collection c =3D cf.generateCertificates(fis);
</PRE>As shown with <CODE>BorderFactory</CODE>, the Factory pattern does =

      not have to be used with the Singleton pattern. However the two =
patterns=20
      are frequently used together. <BR><BR><A=20
      style=3D"FONT-SIZE: 11px; COLOR: rgb(153,153,204); FONT-FAMILY: =
Arial,Verdana,Helvetica,Sans-serif; TEXT-DECORATION: none"=20
      =
href=3D"http://java.sun.com/developer/JDCTechTips/2006/tt0113.html#top">B=
ack=20
      to Top</A> </TD></TR>
