<TABLE cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D580 border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR>
    <TD style=3D"BACKGROUND-COLOR: rgb(237,239,242)" =
width=3D29>&nbsp;</TD>
    <TD=20
    style=3D"FONT-SIZE: 13px; COLOR: rgb(51,51,51); FONT-FAMILY: =
Arial,Verdana,Helvetica,Sans-serif; BACKGROUND-COLOR: rgb(237,239,242)"=20
    vAlign=3Dtop width=3D"100%"><BR><BR>Like the Singleton Pattern, =
which was=20
      covered in the first half of this tip, the Observer pattern is a =
popular=20
      design pattern used in Java programs. The pattern is a behavioral =
design=20
      pattern. It defines a way for classes to be loosely coupled and =
for one=20
      class (or many) to be notified when another is updated. Basically, =
this=20
      means that when something happens in one place, you notify anyone =
who is=20
      observing and that is interested in that one place. <BR><BR>There =
are two=20
      ways to look at the Observer pattern. The first way involves the=20
      <CODE>Observer</CODE> and <CODE>Observable</CODE> classes found in =
the=20
      <CODE>java.util</CODE> package. The second way follows the =
JavaBeans=20
      component model of registering event listeners with components.=20
      <BR><BR>Prior to the creation of the JavaBeans event model, the=20
      <CODE>Observer</CODE> and <CODE>Observable</CODE> classes =
described an=20
      implementation of the Observable pattern. In other words, the =
classes have=20
      been around since the 1.0 version of the Java platform. There is =
nothing=20
      technically wrong with the classes and they are still present in =
the=20
      libraries. The classes still could be used to implement the =
Observable=20
      pattern, but the second model, the JavaBeans component model, is =
typically=20
      used. One significant problem in using the classes to implement =
the=20
      Observable pattern is that you have to extend =
<CODE>Observable</CODE>.=20
      This forces a class hierarchy structure that might not be possible =
in the=20
      single-inheritence world of the Java platform. <BR><BR>The =
JavaBeans=20
      component model of registering event listeners involves a series =
of add=20
      and remove methods, where the listener type is embedded in the =
method=20
      name. For instance, to observe the selection of a button, you =
register an=20
      <CODE>ActionListener</CODE> with the component: <BR><PRE>   =
ActionListener listener =3D new ActionListener() {
      public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent actionEvent) {
          ...
      }
   };=20
   JButton button =3D new JButton("Pick Me");
   button.addActionListener(listener);
</PRE>That really is all there is to the Observer pattern for the=20
      system-defined classes. You implement a listener interface, attach =
it to=20
      the Subject of the observation, and wait. The Subject is what is =
observed.=20
      It is responsible for remembering who is observing. In the =
JavaBeans=20
      component model case, the interface to attach and detach the=20
      <CODE>Observer</CODE> objects is the add/remove listener naming =
pattern.=20
      When the state of the Subject changes, it notifies the=20
      <CODE>Observer</CODE> objects. <BR><BR>One of the main objectives =
of the=20
      pattern is to enable the loose coupling of the Subject and=20
      <CODE>Observer</CODE>. When the <CODE>JButton</CODE> is selected, =
instead=20
      of calling a specific method of a fictitious subclass named=20
      <CODE>ButtonNotification</CODE>, the notification is abstracted =
out into=20
      an interface that anyone can implement. The <CODE>JButton</CODE> =
doesn't=20
      care what class the attached <CODE>Observer</CODE> (listener) is. =
In fact,=20
      the button doesn't care if the implementing class is modified. All =
it=20
      cares about is that the Observer implements a listener. =
<BR><BR>There are=20
      a number of complications you need to watch out for when using the =

      Observer pattern. First is the possibility of a memory leak. A =
reference=20
      to the <CODE>Observer</CODE> is maintained by the Subject. Until =
the=20
      Subject releases the reference, the <CODE>Observer</CODE> cannot =
be=20
      removed by the garbage collector. Be aware of this possibility and =
remove=20
      observers where appropriate. Also note that the set of=20
      <CODE>Observer</CODE> objects is maintained in an unordered =
collection --=20
      at least when registering event listeners. You don't necessarily =
know if=20
      the first registered listener is notified first or last. If you =
need to=20
      have cascading notifications, where object A must be notified =
first,=20
      followed by object B, you must introduce an intermediary object to =
enforce=20
      the ordering. Simply registering the observers in a particular =
order will=20
      not enforce their order of notification. <BR><BR>Another area of =
the Java=20
      platform that models the Observer pattern is the Java Message =
Service=20
      (JMS), with its guaranteed delivery, non-local distribution, and=20
      persistence, to name a few of its benefits. The JMS =
publish-subscribe=20
      messaging model allows any number of subscribers to listen to =
topics of=20
      interest. When a message for the published topic is produced, all =
the=20
      associated subscribers are notified. <BR><BR>There are many other =
places=20
      in the Java platform that model the Observer pattern -- the =
pattern is=20
      frequently used throughout the Java platform. <BR><BR>Since the =
1995=20
      publication of the <EM><A=20
      style=3D"FONT-SIZE: 13px; COLOR: rgb(153,153,204); FONT-FAMILY: =
Arial,Verdana,Helvetica,Sans-serif; TEXT-DECORATION: none"=20
      href=3D"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0201633612/">Design =

      Patterns</A></EM> book by the Gang of Four, other books have been=20
      published that offer different perspectives on the same patterns =
and=20
      introduce additional patterns. Two of the more popular titles are: =

      <UL>
        <LI><A=20
        style=3D"FONT-SIZE: 13px; COLOR: rgb(153,153,204); FONT-FAMILY: =
Arial,Verdana,Helvetica,Sans-serif; TEXT-DECORATION: none"=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0596007124/"><EM>Head =
First=20
        Design Patterns</EM></A>=20
        <LI><A=20
        style=3D"FONT-SIZE: 13px; COLOR: rgb(153,153,204); FONT-FAMILY: =
Arial,Verdana,Helvetica,Sans-serif; TEXT-DECORATION: none"=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0321213351/"><EM>Refactori=
ng=20
        to Patterns</EM></A> </LI></UL>Other books, such as <A=20
      style=3D"FONT-SIZE: 13px; COLOR: rgb(153,153,204); FONT-FAMILY: =
Arial,Verdana,Helvetica,Sans-serif; TEXT-DECORATION: none"=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0321127420/"><EM>Patterns =
of=20
      Enterprise Application Architecture</EM></A> are available for =
those=20
      interested in more targeted design pattern coverage -- in this =
case, for=20
      enterprise applications. <BR><BR>For more information on design =
patterns=20
      in general, see its <A=20
      style=3D"FONT-SIZE: 13px; COLOR: rgb(153,153,204); FONT-FAMILY: =
Arial,Verdana,Helvetica,Sans-serif; TEXT-DECORATION: none"=20
      =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Design_pattern_(computer_science)">W=
ikipedia=20
      entry</A>. <BR><BR>This Tech Tips issue is dedicated to the memory =
of John=20
      Vlissides, one of the original Gang of Four authors. He passed =
away=20
      November 2005. <BR><BR><A=20
      style=3D"FONT-SIZE: 11px; COLOR: rgb(153,153,204); FONT-FAMILY: =
Arial,Verdana,Helvetica,Sans-serif; TEXT-DECORATION: none"=20
      =
href=3D"http://java.sun.com/developer/JDCTechTips/2006/tt0113.html#top">B=
ack=20
      to Top</A> </TD></TR>
